HermesXP — MSN Messenger on Windows XP, talking to your Hermes agent
=====================================================================

This zip has everything needed to get Messenger working on an XP machine.
No internet on the XP is required: the server runs on the Hermes host
(the machine at __LAN_IP__), and this zip came from it.

WHAT'S INSIDE
  HermesXP.exe                 Companion app: tests the connection, and its
                               "Install for MSN" button places the files
                               below and launches Messenger.
  hermesxp.ini                 Connection settings (server __LAN_IP__,
                               owner __OWNER_EMAIL__). Edit if the server
                               moves to another address.
  ca.pem                       The local certificate authority. The Install
                               button puts it in Trusted Root so Messenger
                               trusts the local server's HTTPS login.
  escargot.dll                 Escargot's Switcher: makes Messenger talk
                               to the local server instead of Microsoft.
  msnmsgr.exe-escargot.ini    Switcher settings for Messenger.
  wlcomm.exe-escargot.ini     Switcher settings for its login helper.

INSTALL (about 5 minutes)
  1. Unzip this folder anywhere on the XP (e.g. C:\HermesXP).
  2. Run HermesXP.exe and click "Install for MSN". It copies the DLL and
     settings next to Messenger, installs the CA, and launches Messenger.
  3. ONE MANUAL STEP (no tool can do it safely yet): import escargot.dll
     into BOTH C:\Program Files\Windows Live\Messenger\msnmsgr.exe and
     wlcomm.exe with a PE editor (Stud_PE is the one upstream recommends:
     open the exe, Imports, add escargot.dll, save). Back the exes up first.
  4. Sign in to Messenger as __OWNER_EMAIL__ with the password printed
     when the server was set up. Add the bot (hermes-bot@hermes.local) to
     your contacts and send it a message — Hermes answers.

TROUBLESHOOTING
  - "Connection failed" on Test: check the server IP in hermesxp.ini, and
    that the Hermes host's firewall allows the XP (ports 1863, 443, 18765).
  - Sign-in fails on TLS: the CA may not have installed — run the Install
    button again as an administrator, or install ca.pem by hand into
    Trusted Root Certification Authorities.
  - The zip can also be carried on a USB drive; it contains no passwords.
